  abstract: |
    Abstract. Paper IV.d reported that, on the programme's own instrument, the measured direction of alignment scaling changed for two model families between an unblinded and a blinded protocol. Because the two protocols differ in more than one component, that paper does not pin the change on blinding alone. The standing objection is external validity: the effect might be a property of ARC-Align rather than of AI evaluation generally. This protocol paper fixes, in advance, the one test the programme cannot be accused of designing to its own advantage: the same blinding manipulation run on an established public benchmark the programme did not build, curate or control. Blinded scoring removes the producing model's identity, provider and family markers, self-referential meta-commentary and length cues; unblinded scoring leaves them. The questions preregistered by timestamp are whether blinding changes the score (H1, the per-response difference Delta_S, direction deliberately unregistered after IV.d's own sign reversal), whether rankings displace (H2, Kendall tau), which cue carries the effect (H3, a fractional factorial preregistered by timestamp), and cross-family evaluator agreement (H4, a minimum three-model panel where fewer than three valid scores is missing, never zero). The prediction preregistered by timestamp is a non-zero Delta_S smaller than IV.d observed with modest rank displacement, and the stated most likely outcome is a positive H1 beside a null H2, read as blinding mattering for measurement precision rather than leaderboard order. This is a protocol at the draft-registration stage: preregistered by timestamp and not yet registered on OSF. No data has been collected, and nothing here reports a finding.

